      "type": "explain",
      "id": "e1",
      "title": "Consonants and silent letters",
      "body": "Most French consonants are like English ones. The surprises are the silent final letters and a few letter groups."
    },
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "id": "e2",
      "title": "The big rule: the last consonant is usually silent",
      "body": "petit (puh-TEE), grand (grah(n)), vous (voo), Paris (pah-REE), trop (troh), beaucoup (boh-KOO), chez (shay). The c, r, f, l are often said at the end, so remember CaReFuL: avec (ah-VEK), bonjour (boh(n)-ZHOOR), neuf (nuhf), sel (sel). There are exceptions: -er verbs end in ay (parler is pahR-LAY) and -ier words too."
    },
