      "title": "Watch out",
      "body": "poisson (fish, double s, pwah-SOH(N)) and poison (poison, single s between vowels, pwah-ZOH(N)) differ by one letter. Double s is s. Single s between two vowels is z."
    },
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "id": "e4",
      "title": "In Canada",
      "body": "In Québec, t and d before i or u are often said with a small hiss: tu sounds close to “tsü” and dire close to “dzeer”. That is a normal Québec feature. In your own speech, the standard t and d are always understood, and that is what you practise for the exam."
